---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { html, section } from '../lib/md'
import { article, blob, breadcrumb } from '../lib/site'

const description =
  'How loupe reviews an interface: the profile, category research, measurement, a keyboard walk, stress states, nine passes, the red team, and the rules that decide what counts as a Blocker.'
const rules = html(section('SKILL.md', 'Rules'))
const bar = html(section('references/augment/red-team.md', 'The rework bar'))
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description={description}
  path="/method"
  card="method"
  schema={[breadcrumb(['Method', '/method']), article('How loupe reviews an interface', description, '/method')]}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>How loupe reviews</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      loupe treats Apple’s Human Interface Guidelines as the base and lets other research add to it, never replace it.
      Each outside check says whether it adds to an Apple rule, covers ground Apple leaves open, or conflicts with Apple,
      and a conflict is shown in the finding rather than settled quietly.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What it reads</h2>
    <p>
      The rules come from paraphrased digests of the HIG (snapshot 23 October 2026), Apple’s other design literature
      (WWDC sessions, the Apple Style Guide, the App Review Guidelines, WebKit guidance), and outside research: WCAG 2.2,
      Nielsen Norman Group, Baymard, GOV.UK, Material, Fluent, Carbon and the typography canon. A separate catalogue of
      template tells is dated and kept apart from anything Apple says.
    </p>
    <p>
      A review loads only the files its current pass needs. The skill’s instructions stay under five thousand tokens so
      they survive context compaction in long reviews.
    </p>

    <h2>What it measures</h2>
    <p>
      On a running web app, one call measures the page at 390, 820 and 1440 pixels wide, in light and dark, with and
      without reduced motion: text contrast after compositing every translucent layer, target sizes with the WCAG 2.5.8
      spacing exception, type sizes, motion that ignores the reduced-motion setting, and the defaults that template-built
      pages share. A second script measures the typefaces themselves: whether capital I and lowercase l can be told apart
      at 12 pixels, x-height, tabular figures, and script coverage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then it walks the page with the keyboard, pressing Tab as a person would: the name a screen reader would read at
      each stop, whether focus shows at all, whether a fixed bar hides the focused control, and whether any widget
      keeps focus and won’t let it go. It stresses each screen in place: a 320-pixel window, wider letter and line
      spacing, text at twice the size, text 40% longer with accented letters, and right-to-left, keeping only the
      clipping and overflow each one caused, and undoing each before the next.
    </p>
    <p>
      On Apple platforms it scans the source for text that ignores Dynamic Type, images read aloud by their file name, and
      tap targets VoiceOver doesn’t announce as buttons, and reads the SDK the app was built with to decide which Liquid
      Glass rules apply. It relaunches the simulator with Xcode’s own stress modes: double-length strings,
      right-to-left, the largest text size. For a design system, it checks the colour pairs the components use in every
      mode of the token files.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every report ends with a coverage table: each check family marked measured, read in source, judged by eye, or not
      checked, with the evidence. Anything it can’t measure, it lists under Unverified with what would verify it. The
      same scripts run without Claude on the <a href="/tools">tools page</a> and in CI.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rules-h">The rules, quoted from the skill</h2>
    <p>
      Quoted from <a href={blob('SKILL.md')}>SKILL.md</a> at build time, so this section can’t drift from the skill. It is
      written for the agent, so a few terms need a key:
    </p>
    <dl class="key">
      <dt><code>[Taste]</code></dt>
      <dd>reviewer judgement, labelled as such.</dd>
      <dt>“Apple silent”</dt>
      <dd>Apple has no rule on it; the check comes from outside research.</dd>
      <dt><code>release_stage</code></dt>
      <dd>development or release, from the project’s profile file.</dd>
      <dt><code>(26+)</code></dt>
      <dd>checks that apply only to apps built with the iOS, iPadOS or macOS 26 SDK.</dd>
      <dt>“step 7”</dt>
      <dd>the last step, where it fixes the findings you pick.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
  <details class="prose">
    <summary>The whole Rules section, as the agent reads it</summary>
    <div class="quoted" set:html={rules} />
  </details>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>When it proposes a rework</h2>
    <p>
      A red team that always finds a structural problem is noise. A rework has to clear this bar, from{' '}
      <a href={blob('references/augment/red-team.md')}>red-team.md</a>, and “no rework warranted” is the expected answer
      for a well-built product.
    </p>
  </div>
  <details class="prose">
    <summary>The rework bar, as the agent reads it</summary>
    <div class="quoted" set:html={bar} />
  </details>
</Base>
